## [0012] BUG: all sections below the hero rendered blank

**Reported:** every section under the hero was invisible — white and grey
bands where the plan cards, grid, FAQ and footer should be.

### Root cause (two faults, one mine in process, one mine in design)

**1. Dev server chunks 404'd.** `next build` was run twice against the same
`.next` directory while `next dev` was live. The build replaced the chunks the
running dev server was still serving, so `main-app.js` and
`app-pages-internals.js` returned **404**. React never booted → no `useEffect`
→ no IntersectionObserver, no Lenis → every `.reveal` sat at `opacity: 0`
forever. Confirmed in the network log; `<html>` was also missing Lenis's class,
and 55 of 55 reveals were hidden with 0 marked visible.

**2. The reveal pattern was hidden-by-default.** `.reveal { opacity: 0 }` with
JS as the only path to visibility. That is the real defect: *any* JS failure —
chunk 404, runtime error, disabled JS, a crawler — blanks the entire page. A
marketing page's content must never depend on JS to be visible.

**This was visible earlier and got explained away.** During the initial build,
screenshots of the fine-print, FAQ and footer came back blank and `is-visible`
had to be forced via JS to capture them. That was the bug surfacing; it was
dismissed as scroll timing.

### Fixes

**Fail-safe visibility contract (`app/globals.css`, `app/layout.tsx`)**
- `.reveal` is now **visible by default**. The hidden start state applies only
  under `html[data-reveal='on']`.
- A small inline script in `<head>` sets that attribute before first paint (so
  there is no flash), then removes it after **2500 ms** unless a mounted Reveal
  has set `window.__revealReady`. Hydration fails → attribute drops → all
  content visible, just unanimated.
- `<html suppressHydrationWarning>` because the script mutates it pre-hydration.

Verified on fresh nodes with no transition history:

| State | Computed opacity |
| --- | --- |
| `data-reveal="on"` | `0` |
| `data-reveal="on"` + `.is-visible` | `1` |
| **no `data-reveal`** (JS never booted) | **`1`** |
| previously armed, then disarmed | `1` |

**Reveal driver rewritten (`components/Reveal.tsx`)**
- 55 per-element IntersectionObservers → **one shared registry** with a single
  pass that measures rects. Elements leave the registry once fired, and the
  listeners detach when it empties.
- Coalesced with `queueMicrotask`, not `requestAnimationFrame`: rAF only runs
  while the page paints.
- Listens to `scroll`, `resize` and `visibilitychange`.
- **700 ms timer backstop** while anything is pending. Browsers suspend scroll
  events, rAF and IntersectionObserver in a hidden or occluded tab; timers keep
  running, so nothing can be stranded at opacity 0.

**Process:** never run `next build` while `next dev` is running against the
same `.next`. Stop the dev server, build, then restart.

### Verification

- All dev chunks (`main-app`, `app-pages-internals`, `app/page`, `app/layout`,
  `webpack`) return **200**; previously 404.
- Server-rendered HTML contains **all nine** section headings and **zero**
  inline `opacity:0` — full content without JS, and for crawlers.
- With scroll events, rAF and IntersectionObserver all suspended (hidden tab),
  the timer backstop alone still marked **48 of 55** reveals visible.
- `tsc --noEmit` clean; `next build` static at 8.15 kB / 110 kB.

**Note on measuring this:** in a hidden tab, CSS transitions do not advance, so
an element can carry `.is-visible` while `getComputedStyle().opacity` still
reads `0`. Assert on the `is-visible` class, not computed opacity, when the
pane is not painting — computed opacity there measures the browser's paint
state, not the app's logic.

