## [0017] Full-bleed background sections, done correctly

A reference screenshot settled it: cable and home phone are back on
`layout: 'background'`, and the piece I had missing was the scrim.

### What the earlier attempt got wrong

Entry [0015] took "don't put any overlay" literally and ran the photograph
undimmed, which forced two bad consequences: the copy had to move onto floating
white panels, and the 1250px source's ~1.5x upscale had nothing hiding it, so
it looked visibly blown up.

The reference does the opposite — the photograph is **clearly darkened**, with
centred white type directly on it. The scrim is doing two jobs at once: it
carries the type, and it conceals the upscale. Remove it and both problems
appear together, which is exactly what happened.

So the resolution ceiling flagged in [0016] was real but not the blocker.
1250px is fine edge to edge *under a scrim*; it is not fine bare.

### Implementation

- Section is `bg-arvig-900` with the image at `fill` / `object-cover`, then a
  gradient scrim `from-arvig-900/70 via-arvig-900/78 to-arvig-900/88`.
- New `headerOnDark`: lime eyebrow, centred white `h2`, centred `arvig-100`
  intro, capped at `max-w-[760px]`.
- Cards centre below the copy — `max-w-[520px]` for one plan, `max-w-[880px]`
  for two. They are already opaque white, so they read as they do in the
  reference.
- Footnote, if present, centres in `arvig-200`.
- `objectPosition`: cable `center 55%`, phone `62% center`.

### Contrast

Worst case is the cable shot's overcast sky (~200,200,204) under the 78% stop:
composite `#3f3576`, white on it **≈10.6:1**. Even at the lightest 70% stop it
is ≈8.6:1. Measured in-browser: heading `rgb(255,255,255)`, intro
`rgb(232,231,239)`, cards `rgb(255,255,255)`, section base `rgb(29,16,96)` —
the official Arvig indigo.

### Verification

- At **320px**: no horizontal scroll, **0 gutter violations**.
- `tsc --noEmit` clean; `next build` static.

---

## [0018] Scrims reduced across hero, cable and home phone

All three photographic scrims lightened. Levels were chosen by computing
contrast at candidate values rather than by eye, against the brightest
backdrop that actually sits behind text in each image.

| Surface | Before | After |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Hero | `38 / 55 / 85` | **`28 / 50 / 78`** |
| Cable + home phone | `70 / 78 / 88` | **`50 / 58 / 74`** |

### Two text colours were forced by the measurements

Lightening a scrim raises the luminance behind the type, so two existing
choices stopped clearing AA and had to move. Both were passing before only
because the scrims were dark.

**Lime eyebrow on the background sections → white.** Against the cable shot's
overcast sky (~200,200,204), lime `#C0D52F` measures:

| scrim | 44% | 48% | 52% | 56% | 60% |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| lime | 2.56 | 2.83 | 3.12 | 3.46 | 3.84 |

It fails at every level under 66%, so it could not survive the reduction. White
at the 50% top stop gives ~4.8:1. The reference screenshot uses a muted
non-accent eyebrow too, so this also matches it more closely.

**Hero subline `arvig-200` → `arvig-100`.** Over the sunset strip
(~215,175,140), `arvig-200` measures 3.04–3.93:1 across the candidate range —
failing everywhere. `arvig-100` reaches 4.57:1 at the 50% mid stop, which set
the floor for that stop.

### Resulting margins

- Hero, 50% mid over sunset: white **5.6:1**, arvig-100 **4.6:1**.
- Background sections, 58% mid over overcast sky: white **5.9:1**,
  arvig-100 **4.8:1**.

Going lighter than these values drops the intro paragraphs below AA, so this is
the floor for the current photography.

### Verification

Rendered scrims confirmed in-browser at `0.28/0.5/0.78` and `0.5/0.58/0.74`;
hero subline and section intros `rgb(232,231,239)`, section eyebrows
`rgb(255,255,255)`. At **320px**: no horizontal scroll, **0 gutter violations**.
`tsc --noEmit` clean; `next build` static.

