## [0019] Hero emphasis, Why Us on photography, hero scrim reduced again

### Headline highlight

`headlineHighlight: 'high-speed internet'` added to `hero` in
`lib/content.ts`, and the Hero splits the headline around it and wraps that
span in the Arvig lime. Kept as data so the emphasis travels with the copy
instead of being hard-coded in JSX; if the phrase is ever edited out of the
headline the split falls back to rendering it plain.

Confirmed rendering as `rgb(192, 213, 47)` — the official `#C0D52F`.

### Plan lockup promoted

The hero price anchor now sits in its own container: `rounded-[18px]`, a
`border-lime-brand/45` outline, `bg-arvig-950/45`, a soft drop shadow and a
light backdrop blur. It reads as a distinct offer rather than loose text.

### Why Us moved onto the photograph

`why-us-local-team.jpg` was a boxed banner between the heading and the cards;
it is now the section's full-bleed background, matching cable and home phone.
Same scrim levels (`50 / 58 / 74`), centred white eyebrow and heading.

The six claim cards changed from opaque white to
`bg-arvig-950/62` + `border-white/14` + a light blur. Two reasons: six opaque
white cards would have covered the photograph almost entirely, and a
self-darkening card holds its contrast wherever the picture happens to be
bright. Measured over the brightest part of that shot: white **11.6:1**,
arvig-100 body **9.4:1**.

### Hero scrim — past the previous floor, via a pool

Entry [0018] recorded `28 / 50 / 78` as the floor, because lightening the flat
gradient any further dropped the subline below AA. That was true of a *flat*
gradient. Concentrating the density where the text actually sits lifts the
ceiling:

- flat gradient reduced to **`16 / 30 / 66`**
- plus a radial pool, `radial-gradient(74% 62% at 50% 44%, …0.44 → …0.18 →
  transparent)`

| | Before | Now |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Photograph edges | 50% | **30%** |
| Composite behind the type | 50% | **60.8%** |

So the picture runs considerably brighter while contrast behind the copy
actually improved. Over the sunset strip, the worst backdrop: white
**7.2:1**, arvig-100 **5.9:1**, lime highlight **4.4:1** — and the highlight
is 3.5rem, which is large text needing only 3:1.

### Verification

At **320px**: no horizontal scroll, **0 gutter violations**; the new plan
container spans 20–300, exactly inside the gutters. `tsc --noEmit` clean;
`next build` static.

