## [0020] Hero motion + gradient headline, scrim reduced again

Note this deliberately goes past the original brief's "minimal, refined
animations only" constraint, at the user's request. Everything added is slow
and continuous rather than attention-seeking, and no WebGL, 3D or assembly
animation was introduced — those negative constraints still hold.

### Gradient headline

The accented phrase now renders as a gradient
(`from-lime-brand via-white to-lime-brand`) clipped to the text, with a 7s
linear sheen sweeping through it via `background-position`.

Wrapped in an outer `<span className="text-lime-brand">` so the phrase still
reads as solid lime if `background-clip: text` is unsupported — a transparent
text fill with no clip support would otherwise render invisible.

### Background motion

| Animation | Duration | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `hero-pan` | 34s, alternate | Ken Burns on the backdrop. Starts at `scale(1.06)` so the translate can never expose an edge |
| `hero-drift-a` | 19s | White wash, upper left |
| `hero-drift-b` | 26s | Lime wash, upper right |
| `text-sheen` | 7s | Sheen through the headline gradient |
| `hero-rise` | 0.85s, once | Staggered entrance: 0 / 90 / 180 / 280 / 380 / 470 ms |

**The washes had to be cut back sharply.** At the first attempt
(`bg-white/10`, `bg-lime-brand/10`, 55% and 50% wide, peaking at 0.8 and 0.6
opacity) the hero looked *worse* — a wash sits on top of the photograph, so it
lifts the blacks and flattens the picture into haze rather than brightening
it. Reduced to `bg-white/6` and `bg-lime-brand/7`, 38% and 34% wide, peaking at
0.55 and 0.45, and confined to the upper corners away from the house.

### Scrim reduced again

| | Before | Now |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Flat gradient | `16 / 30 / 66` | **`12 / 24 / 58`** |
| Radial pool | 0.44 → 0.18 | **0.48 → 0.20** |
| Photograph edges | 30% | **24%** |
| Composite behind type | 60.8% | **60.5%** |

Deepening the pool while lightening the flat layer keeps the contrast behind
the copy effectively unchanged while opening the picture up further. Over the
sunset strip: white **7.2:1**, arvig-100 **5.9:1**, lime **4.4:1** (3.5rem, so
large text needing 3:1).

### Reduced motion

All five animations are switched off outright under
`prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` rather than compressed to 1ms, since they are
purely decorative. Confirmed present in the served stylesheet.

### Verification note

All five keyframes and the reduced-motion block were confirmed in the served
CSS, and every animation reports the correct name, duration, iteration count
and `playState: "running"`.

**The motion itself could not be watched.** The preview pane is not producing
frames — `document.hidden` reads false, but the document timeline is frozen
(`currentTime` static at 11344ms across a 1200ms wait). Non-zero timeline
values and a mid-sweep `background-position` of -124% show the animations did
run while the pane was painting. They should be eyeballed in a real browser.

### Verification

At **320px**: no horizontal scroll, **0 gutter violations**, h1 spanning
20–300. `tsc --noEmit` clean; `next build` static.

