## [0022] Mobile responsiveness pass, repo, Vercel readiness

### Audit method

Rather than resizing the pane nine times, the page was loaded into same-origin
iframes at nine widths (320, 360, 390, 414, 480, 640, 768, 1024, 1280) and
measured from the parent document — overflow, gutter breaches, touch-target
heights and computed font sizes, all in a few calls.

**Horizontal overflow: none at any of the nine widths**, before or after the
changes. That part was already sound.

### Real defects found and fixed

**iOS Safari zoom on focus.** The ZIP input rendered at **15px**. Safari zooms
the entire page whenever a focused input is under 16px, which on a phone throws
the layout off and strands the user mid-page. Now `text-[1rem]` with
`sm:text-[0.9375rem]`, so it is 16px exactly where it matters. Confirmed
`16px` at 320 and 390.

**Touch targets.** 20–23 controls measured under 40px, several under WCAG
2.5.8's 24px floor: footer links at **17px**, desktop nav links at 23px, the
mobile Call button at 38px. Fixed with `inline-block py-1.5` on footer links,
`py-1` on nav links and `!py-[13px]` on the mobile Call button. Now
**0 targets under 24px** at every width.

**11px type.** 55 instances of `text-[0.6875rem]`, mostly uppercase labels, in
the fine-print grid, footer headings and the "Most popular" badge. Raised to
12px on phones and left at 11px from `sm` up where the viewing distance suits
it.

**Mobile motion budget.** Two animated elements carried `blur(130px)` and
`blur(140px)` — among the most expensive things a phone GPU composites, and
barely perceptible at that width. Below `sm` the drifts stop animating and
their blur halves to 65px. The Ken Burns pan stays (a composited transform on
one layer is cheap) but its travel shortens so it cannot crop the house out of
a narrow frame. Verified: `animation-name: none`, `filter: blur(65px)` at
320px.

**Hero height.** 1292px at 320px, roughly two screens before any plan. Mobile
padding and inter-element margins tightened; now 1226px at 320 and 1097px at
390.

### Repository

`git init`, branch `main`, one commit, remote
`https://github.com/PavithraEbbali/arvig.git`.

- `.gitignore` excludes `assets-source/` — the 30 MB of uncropped PNG masters.
  The shipped 884 KB of optimised JPEGs in `public/images` **are** committed.
- `.gitattributes` normalises to LF, since Vercel builds on Linux.
- A stray `arvig.html` (the Arvig homepage, downloaded during colour
  extraction) was caught during staging and removed.
- 35 files committed.

### Vercel

No configuration needed — the framework is auto-detected and the defaults are
correct. No environment variables. `next/image` optimisation works natively on
Vercel, so the photography is served as WebP/AVIF per breakpoint.

### Push not completed

`gh` is not installed and Git Credential Manager holds no stored GitHub
credential, so `git push` fails with `could not read Username`. Entering a
token or password is not something to do on the user's behalf. The commit,
branch and remote are all in place; the push is one authenticated command away.

`tsc --noEmit` clean; `next build` static at 14.3 kB / 116 kB.

